linux-user: pass personality's argument as the 32-bit value the kernel uses

For 32-bit guests, personality(0xffffffff) (a query, which bionic makes at
startup) reached the host sign-extended to 0xffffffffffffffff. The kernel
only looks at the low 32 bits, but seccomp filters that allow personality
for specific values (like Docker's default profile) compare all 64 bits and
reject it, so the guest gets ENOSYS.

--- a/linux-user/syscall.c
+++ b/linux-user/syscall.c
@@ -11843,7 +11843,7 @@
     case TARGET_NR_fchdir:
         return get_errno(fchdir(arg1));
     case TARGET_NR_personality:
-        return get_errno(personality(arg1));
+        return get_errno(personality((abi_uint)arg1));
 #ifdef TARGET_NR__llseek /* Not on alpha */
     case TARGET_NR__llseek:
         {
